The master of D₀ meet combinedX at i is contained in combinedX at j if and only if the master of D₁ meet combinedY at i is contained in combinedY at j.

Emptiness transfer at the constraints that affirm i and deny j, with bound one more than their maximum, equates nonemptiness of the two described sets. Each described set is the corresponding master intersection minus the set at j. Emptiness of a difference is containment, so negating both sides yields the two containments.
